//! check: run(exit=0, stdout="ok")
//! phase: run
//! conforms: gram.lex.str.multi, gram.lex.str.escape
//!
//! Issue #215, the running half. `[gram.lex.str.multi]` said
//! "Interpolation works inside" and nothing whatsoever about escapes,
//! while its two neighbours say the opposite outright — raw is "no
//! escapes, no interpolation", generalized is a "raw-mode body". Read
//! against them the silence reads as "escapes work", which is the
//! reading tree-sitter-wolf took at le05 while deciding whether its new
//! `invalid_escape` node belongs inside `"""…"""`. That is an inference
//! from an omission, in a spec whose own #198 fix was to stop asserting
//! escape facts in prose and start deriving them.
//!
//! The silence was right, and it is a production now:
//! `MULTI_PART ::= MULTI_TEXT | STR_ESC | '{{' | '}}' | INTERP` — the
//! same alternatives `STR_PART` has, because the lexer scans a multiline
//! body with the same routine as a plain one, with the line break and
//! the lone `"` admitted as text. This file measures it: `\t`, `\u{…}`
//! at six digits, `\"` (which does NOT close the literal), and the `{{`
//! `}}` literal braces — inside `"""…"""`, compared against the same
//! text written as a plain string. Its refusal twin is
//! `grammar/multiline_bad_escape.lu`.
//!
//! Interpolation is deliberately absent: it lexes here (INTERP is a
//! `MULTI_PART`) and the mid-end refuses it by name — "interpolation
//! inside a multiline string (s38 formatting)" — so a witness carrying
//! one would measure the conservatism ledger, not the grammar.

fn main() -> !int {
    let s = """
        tab:\there
        wolf:\u{01F43A}
        quote:\" brace:{{}}
        """
    let want = "tab:\there\nwolf:\u{01F43A}\nquote:\" brace:{{}}\n"
    if s == want {
        print("ok")
        0
    } else {
        print("GOT {s}")
        1
    }
}
